Looking at this problem, I need to prove $\int_0^1\int_0^1 |f(x)+f(y)|\,dx\,dy \geq \int_0^1 |f(x)|\,dx$ for continuous $f$ on $[0,1]$.

**Proof.**

Let $A = \{x \in [0,1] : f(x) \geq 0\}$ and $B = \{x \in [0,1] : f(x) < 0\}$, with $\alpha = m(A)$, $\beta = m(B)$, so $\alpha + \beta = 1$. Set
$$a = \int_A f(x)\,dx = \int_0^1 f^+(x)\,dx \geq 0, \qquad b = \int_B |f(x)|\,dx = \int_0^1 f^-(x)\,dx \geq 0.$$
Then $\int_0^1 |f(x)|\,dx = a + b$, which is our target.

**Decomposing the double integral.** On $A \times A$, $f(x)+f(y) \geq 0$ so $|f(x)+f(y)| = f(x)+f(y)$. On $B \times B$, $f(x)+f(y) < 0$ so $|f(x)+f(y)| = |f(x)|+|f(y)|$. On $A\times B$ (and symmetrically $B\times A$), we keep the absolute value. By symmetry:

$$\iint |f(x)+f(y)|\,dx\,dy = \underbrace{2\alpha a}_{A\times A} + \underbrace{2\beta b}_{B\times B} + \underbrace{2\int_A\int_B |f(x)+f(y)|\,dx\,dy}_{\text{cross}}.$$

**Lower-bounding the cross term.** For $(x,y)\in A\times B$: $f(x)\geq 0$, $f(y)=-|f(y)|$, so $f(x)+f(y)=f(x)-|f(y)|$, giving
$$|f(x)+f(y)| = |f(x)-|f(y)|| \geq f(x)-|f(y)| \quad\text{and}\quad \geq |f(y)|-f(x).$$
Integrating over $A\times B$:
$$\int_A\int_B |f(x)+f(y)|\,dx\,dy \geq \int_A\int_B\bigl(f(x)-|f(y)|\bigr)\,dx\,dy = a\beta - \alpha b, \tag{$\star_1$}$$
$$\int_A\int_B |f(x)+f(y)|\,dx\,dy \geq \int_A\int_B\bigl(|f(y)|-f(x)\bigr)\,dx\,dy = \alpha b - \beta a. \tag{$\star_2$}$$

**Two lower bounds for the LHS.** Using $(\star_1)$:
$$\text{LHS} \geq 2\alpha a + 2\beta b + 2(\beta a - \alpha b) = 2a + 2b(1-2\alpha). \tag{Bound 1}$$
Using $(\star_2)$:
$$\text{LHS} \geq 2\alpha a + 2\beta b + 2(\alpha b - \beta a) = 2a(2\alpha-1) + 2b. \tag{Bound 2}$$

**Showing $\max(\text{Bound 1}, \text{Bound 2}) \geq a+b$.**

*Bound 1 $\geq a+b$* $\iff$ $a + b(1-2\alpha) \geq 0$. This is automatic when $\alpha \leq \tfrac{1}{2}$ (both terms non-negative). When $\alpha > \tfrac{1}{2}$, it requires $a \geq (2\alpha-1)b$.

*Bound 2 $\geq a+b$* $\iff$ $(4\alpha-3)a + b \geq 0$. This is automatic when $\alpha \geq \tfrac{3}{4}$ (since $4\alpha-3\geq 0$). When $\alpha < \tfrac{3}{4}$, it requires $b \geq (3-4\alpha)a$.

**Case 1:** $\alpha \leq \tfrac{1}{2}$. Bound 1 gives LHS $\geq a+b$. ✓

**Case 2:** $\alpha \geq \tfrac{3}{4}$. Bound 2 gives LHS $\geq a+b$. ✓

**Case 3:** $\tfrac{1}{2} < \alpha < \tfrac{3}{4}$. Suppose for contradiction that **both** bounds fail:
$$a < (2\alpha - 1)b \qquad\text{and}\qquad b < (3 - 4\alpha)\,a.$$
If $b = 0$ then $a < 0$, contradiction; so $b > 0$. From the second inequality, $a > \frac{b}{3-4\alpha}$. Combining:
$$\frac{b}{3-4\alpha} < (2\alpha-1)\,b \implies \frac{1}{3-4\alpha} < 2\alpha - 1 \implies 1 < (2\alpha-1)(3-4\alpha).$$
Set $s = 2\alpha - 1 \in (0,1)$. Then $3 - 4\alpha = 1 - 2s$, so the condition becomes $1 < s(1-2s) = s - 2s^2$, i.e., $2s^2 - s + 1 < 0$. But the discriminant is $1 - 8 = -7 < 0$, so $2s^2 - s + 1 > 0$ for all real $s$. **Contradiction.**

Therefore at least one bound always holds, giving LHS $\geq a + b = \int_0^1 |f(x)|\,dx$. $\blacksquare$

